// Shared data-accuracy points, used on /terms/ (as a section) and on /disclaimer/ so the two never drift apart.
---
<ul>
  <li><strong>The grades come from the tire makers.</strong> UTQG treadwear, traction and temperature grades are assigned by each tire maker and reported to the U.S. government (NHTSA). We republish NHTSA’s public data. We do not test tires, and we have not independently verified the grades.</li>
  <li><strong>Treadwear grades are only comparable within the same maker.</strong> Each maker runs its own tests, so a treadwear 600 from one maker does not necessarily wear the same as a 600 from another. Treadwear is also not a mileage guarantee; real-world wear depends on the vehicle, driving, climate, road surface, inflation, alignment and rotation.</li>
  <li><strong>The data can be incomplete, wrong or out of date.</strong> NHTSA records can have gaps or errors, and tire makers change or discontinue products. Every page shows the date the data was pulled.</li>
  <li><strong>Prices and warranties change.</strong> This site does not show prices. Any maker warranty figure we show is the maker’s own statement as of the date shown; check the maker’s current terms and the retailer’s current price before you buy.</li>
  <li><strong>The cost-per-mile calculator is an estimate.</strong> It does arithmetic on the numbers you type in. It does not know your vehicle, your driving or the actual life of a tire.</li>
  <li><strong>Always follow the vehicle and tire professionals.</strong> Use the tire size, load index, speed rating and inflation pressure shown on your vehicle’s tire placard (usually on the driver’s door edge or post) and owner’s manual, read the information molded into the tire sidewall, and ask a qualified tire professional if you are unsure what is safe for your vehicle. Have tires inspected regularly for wear, damage and age.</li>
</ul>
